## Proof

Let

$$I = \int_0^{+\infty} \frac{\log t}{1+t^2}\,dt.$$

**Convergence.** Near $t=0$, $\log t$ is integrable since $\int_0^1 |\log t|\,dt = 1 < \infty$, and $1/(1+t^2)\to 1$, so the integrand behaves like $\log t$. Near $t=+\infty$, the integrand behaves like $\log t / t^2$, and $\int_1^\infty \frac{\log t}{t^2}\,dt = 1 < \infty$. Hence $I$ converges.

**Substitution $t \mapsto 1/u$.** Set $t = 1/u$, so $dt = -du/u^2$. The limits transform as $t\to 0^+ \Rightarrow u\to +\infty$ and $t\to+\infty \Rightarrow u\to 0^+$. Therefore

$$
I = \int_{+\infty}^{0} \frac{\log(1/u)}{1+1/u^2}\left(-\frac{du}{u^2}\right)
  = \int_0^{+\infty} \frac{-\log u}{(u^2+1)/u^2}\cdot\frac{du}{u^2}
  = \int_0^{+\infty} \frac{-\log u}{1+u^2}\,du
  = -I.
$$

**Conclusion.** We have shown $I = -I$, so $2I = 0$, and thus

$$\int_0^{+\infty} \frac{\log t}{1+t^2}\,dt = 0.$$

